EXPERIMENT (never lands): Linux v6.1 (830b3c68c1fb, the kit Image's source) + Capstone tags follow pages.
Apply with patch -p1 in a v6.1 tree; build with build-kernel.sh and the kit .config (9c724799).

EXPERIMENT (never lands): riscv: Capstone capability tags follow pages

Capstone's virtual profile keeps a capability's tag with the physical granule. Linux copies
user pages (migration and compaction through folio_copy, COW in wp_page_copy and fork's
copy_present_page, THP collapse, hugetlb) with scalar stores, so the copy comes out
untagged, and a freed page keeps its tags for its next owner. The pinned capstone_vm module
avoids both by pinning every page a domain reaches and sweeping it at retire; this is the
other design, so that domain pages need no pin:

- copy_highpage / copy_user_highpage (arch versions): copy the bytes, then give the
  destination exactly the source's tags;
- arch_free_page: clear the tags of every freed page, any order;
- arch_prepare_to_swap: refuse the swap-out of a page holding a tag (it stays in the
  swap cache with its tags; no swap-side tag store exists).

The moves use the test-only tag-move CSRs 0x5c8-0x5cb of capstone-qemu experiment/tag-copy
(x-capstone-test-csrs), a stand-in for the raw tag moves the ISA does not have; an
instruction count prices each at one CSR access. Counts of copies, frees and refused
swap-outs are in /sys/module/captag/parameters; captag=off restores stock behaviour.

